WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.037
Welcome to Building HR-Driven Identity.

2
00:00:03.037 --> 00:00:06.100
This is Chapter 1: the
writer register as code.

3
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.462
A rule that reads an attribute hands
the decision to whoever can write it.

4
00:00:10.462 --> 00:00:14.801
The register names that writer, and the
audit log says whether anyone else did.

5
00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:18.787
This chapter keeps the register's
lifecycle rows as a reviewed file,

6
00:00:18.787 --> 00:00:22.346
checks its shape, and scans the
directory audit log for every

7
00:00:22.346 --> 00:00:26.475
protected write that didn't come from
the named writer or its backstop.

8
00:00:26.475 --> 00:00:30.488
We'll see what Microsoft Learn
gives you, what it leaves to you,

9
00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:33.900
the register, the scan,
two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.362
Why does this gate come first?

11
00:00:39.362 --> 00:00:41.500
Because every later gate leans on it.

12
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.650
The guide's first step names one
writer for every state and value.

13
00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.692
Without that, a late active value of true
from the pipeline undoes a workflow's

14
00:00:50.692 --> 00:00:53.765
disable, and a help desk
edit to an organization

15
00:00:53.765 --> 00:00:56.838
code moves a person's
access without HR knowing.

16
00:00:56.838 --> 00:00:58.876
Book 3 explains the design.

17
00:00:58.876 --> 00:01:01.010
This chapter makes it checkable.

18
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.510
Which cloud matters for this read.

19
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.563
The scan reads two Graph collections, the
directory audits and the provisioning log,

20
00:01:11.563 --> 00:01:16.110
and both are listed for the global
service and for both US Government levels.

21
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:22.172
G C C uses the global Graph host; G
C C High and level 5 have their own.

22
00:01:22.172 --> 00:01:26.234
The export script takes the cloud
as an argument and has no default.

23
00:01:26.234 --> 00:01:30.484
And Entra keeps audit records for
only thirty days with P1 or P2,

24
00:01:30.484 --> 00:01:34.570
so the export has to run on a
schedule well inside that window,

25
00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:38.610
with the logs already flowing
to the agency's own store.

26
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.360
Start with Microsoft's procedure.

27
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.518
Learn's list directory
audits page gives the read,

28
00:01:47.518 --> 00:01:52.448
its least-privileged permission, audit
log read all, and the filter operators.

29
00:01:52.448 --> 00:01:56.872
Its example shows the record: the
activity name and time, the result,

30
00:01:56.872 --> 00:02:01.458
who initiated it, either an app with
its service principal ID or a user,

31
00:02:01.458 --> 00:02:04.435
and the target with its
modified properties.

32
00:02:04.435 --> 00:02:08.244
The audit activity reference
names the six user activities

33
00:02:08.244 --> 00:02:11.672
this chapter watches, from
add user to set user manager.

34
00:02:11.672 --> 00:02:14.723
What Learn doesn't give is a
test of who may write what.

35
00:02:14.723 --> 00:02:17.190
There's no Microsoft sample for that.

36
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.840
Here's what Learn leaves to
you, and what the kit adds.

37
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.014
The log records every
write and its initiator,

38
00:02:27.014 --> 00:02:31.440
but not which identity may write
which value: that's the register.

39
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.794
One enable activity covers
two lifecycle rows,

40
00:02:34.794 --> 00:02:38.578
hire and cancellation, which
must agree on one writer.

41
00:02:38.578 --> 00:02:43.791
Every change is logged, but testing each
successful protected write is yours.

42
00:02:43.791 --> 00:02:48.753
So are the saved queries, least privilege
per task, and evidence nobody edited.

43
00:02:48.753 --> 00:02:51.267
The verdict: use Learn's API as it is,

44
00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:55.030
and build the register
and the test against it.

45
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.642
The register holds the
guide's lifecycle rows.

46
00:03:01.642 --> 00:03:04.003
Each row names the audited activity,

47
00:03:04.003 --> 00:03:07.543
and for an update user record,
the property it protects.

48
00:03:07.543 --> 00:03:10.070
It names one writer, an optional backstop,

49
00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.342
and for deletion, a list of
writers that are forbidden.

50
00:03:13.342 --> 00:03:18.580
Writers are named groups of service principal
IDs: inbound provisioning, Lifecycle

51
00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:23.218
Workflows, the card system's binding
writer, and the interim directory sync.

52
00:03:23.218 --> 00:03:26.731
One caution: the property names
aren't documented on Learn.

53
00:03:26.731 --> 00:03:30.760
They're the program's best
reading, and Lab 2 confirms them.

54
00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.498
The check reads the register's shape.

55
00:03:36.498 --> 00:03:41.285
The first rule flags a row without a
single writer, or a row listed twice.

56
00:03:41.285 --> 00:03:43.840
Others flag a writer that isn't defined,

57
00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.673
or one with no identities, so
its writes can't be attributed.

58
00:03:47.673 --> 00:03:51.648
A required account-state transition
with no row is a finding.

59
00:03:51.648 --> 00:03:53.895
So is deletion by inbound provisioning,

60
00:03:53.895 --> 00:03:57.435
since the guide deletes through
the post-separation workflow.

61
00:03:57.435 --> 00:03:59.756
A backstop can't be the writer itself,

62
00:03:59.756 --> 00:04:02.773
and rows for the same
audited change must agree.

63
00:04:02.773 --> 00:04:07.380
An unsigned register and an
unversioned scope filter are warnings.

64
00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:15.367
The scan tests every protected write
in the window against the register.

65
00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:18.553
A write by an identity that's
neither the writer nor

66
00:04:18.553 --> 00:04:22.042
the backstop is a finding:
that's the unauthorized write.

67
00:04:22.042 --> 00:04:26.468
A write by the backstop is a warning,
because the writer should have acted first.

68
00:04:26.468 --> 00:04:29.929
And a write by a writer the
row forbids is a finding.

69
00:04:29.929 --> 00:04:33.764
The scan reads only successful
records inside the window,

70
00:04:33.764 --> 00:04:38.796
takes the first app or user as the
initiator, and counts an enable record once,

71
00:04:38.796 --> 00:04:42.580
not against both the hire
and the cancellation rows.

72
00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:51.201
The writers scan task needs two permissions,
each the least-privileged for its call:

73
00:04:51.201 --> 00:04:53.742
audit log read all for
the directory audits,

74
00:04:53.742 --> 00:04:57.130
and that plus directory read
all for the provisioning log.

75
00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.280
Both are reads.

76
00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.193
And here's what the scan can't see.

77
00:05:00.193 --> 00:05:03.467
The Entra log records
writes made in Entra I D.

78
00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:07.524
For synced users, the slots are
mastered in Active Directory,

79
00:05:07.524 --> 00:05:10.931
and those writes show up
only in A D change auditing.

80
00:05:10.931 --> 00:05:14.463
So the register names the sync
agent as the interim writer,

81
00:05:14.463 --> 00:05:17.920
and the O U permissions
are reviewed every quarter.

82
00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:23.958
The tests hold all of this in place.

83
00:05:23.958 --> 00:05:29.093
Nine belong to this chapter: the shipped
register is clean, the shape rules work,

84
00:05:29.093 --> 00:05:32.253
rows for one change agree,
one enable counts once,

85
00:05:32.253 --> 00:05:36.440
second writers and the forbidden
delete are found, and failures,

86
00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.707
unprotected changes and records
outside the window are ignored.

87
00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:43.964
Ten more are shared: the
call list, the cloud table,

88
00:05:43.964 --> 00:05:47.220
the output folder and the
export that only reads.

89
00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.449
All fifty-seven tests in the kit
pass under Python 3.10 or later,

90
00:05:52.449 --> 00:05:55.690
with no package, tenant or network.

91
00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.015
The verified facts, each
read on November first.

92
00:06:03.015 --> 00:06:08.486
The directory audits list is version 1.0
and listed for both government levels,

93
00:06:08.486 --> 00:06:11.853
with the filter operators
the saved queries use.

94
00:06:11.853 --> 00:06:15.541
The six user activities come from
the Core Directory reference.

95
00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.953
Retention is thirty days,
and it isn't retroactive.

96
00:06:18.953 --> 00:06:21.722
And two places Learn says
nothing are recorded,

97
00:06:21.722 --> 00:06:26.056
so a later edit doesn't loosen the code:
the display name each property gets

98
00:06:26.056 --> 00:06:30.960
in the modified properties, and the
service principal each service writes as.

99
00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.710
What does this prove, and for whom?

100
00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:40.475
The register's rows, version,
signature and scope filter,

101
00:06:40.475 --> 00:06:44.813
checked by the nine register
rules, support account management

102
00:06:44.813 --> 00:06:48.660
and identifier management,
AC-2 and IA-4, at Moderate.

103
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.635
The I S S O keeps the signed
register and the check's output.

104
00:06:52.635 --> 00:06:58.143
The scan's findings support audit review,
AU-6, for writes outside the named writer;

105
00:06:58.143 --> 00:07:00.938
keep the output folder with its manifest,

106
00:07:00.938 --> 00:07:06.035
the saved queries and the A D auditing
settings, which this code doesn't read.

107
00:07:06.035 --> 00:07:10.960
And the privacy basis for the feed comes
from the privacy office, not from code.

108
00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:15.635
Who does what?

109
00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:18.583
Developers build the
HR lifecycle's checks;

110
00:07:18.583 --> 00:07:23.497
they need to read Python and JSON, and
they don't need a tenant until Lab 2.

111
00:07:23.497 --> 00:07:25.886
The data architect owns the register.

112
00:07:25.886 --> 00:07:31.192
Identity engineers remove the other write
paths, which is how every second-writer

113
00:07:31.192 --> 00:07:35.485
finding closes: by removing the
write path, not by editing the value.

114
00:07:35.485 --> 00:07:38.080
And the I S S O keeps the signed register,

115
00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.340
the check's output, and the
scan's output folder as evidence.

116
00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:49.940
Lab 1 runs on a laptop,
in the H R lifecycle kit.

117
00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:53.422
Step one lists what the
scan reads: two GET calls,

118
00:07:53.422 --> 00:07:56.815
with audit log read all
and directory read all.

119
00:07:56.815 --> 00:08:00.940
Step two checks the register:
no findings, no warnings.

120
00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:03.915
Step three scans October's audit records.

121
00:08:03.915 --> 00:08:07.350
It counts eleven protected
writes: five by the writer,

122
00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:11.253
one by the backstop, four by a
second writer and one forbidden.

123
00:08:11.253 --> 00:08:15.263
That's five findings and one
warning, the exit code is two,

124
00:08:15.263 --> 00:08:18.500
and the manifest lists every file's hash.

125
00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:24.325
Step four reads the findings.

126
00:08:24.325 --> 00:08:27.892
A help desk administrator
disabled a user at separation,

127
00:08:27.892 --> 00:08:29.713
and edited another's manager.

128
00:08:29.713 --> 00:08:31.788
Another account wrote a leave date.

129
00:08:31.788 --> 00:08:34.613
A fix-up script wrote
an organization code.

130
00:08:34.613 --> 00:08:36.350
All four are second writers.

131
00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:40.600
Inbound provisioning deleted an
account, which the register forbids.

132
00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:44.594
And Lifecycle Workflows, the
backstop, enabled a new hire,

133
00:08:44.594 --> 00:08:47.313
so confirm the writer acted or was late.

134
00:08:47.313 --> 00:08:50.681
Three records aren't counted:
an unprotected change,

135
00:08:50.681 --> 00:08:53.501
a failed disable and
one outside the window.

136
00:08:53.501 --> 00:08:59.050
Step five reads a saved query, and
step six: fifty-seven tests pass.

137
00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.685
Lab 2 uses a test tenant in
the worldwide or G C C cloud,

138
00:09:06.685 --> 00:09:09.938
never production; the
authors haven't run it.

139
00:09:09.938 --> 00:09:14.701
Register a reader app with a certificate
and exactly the two read permissions.

140
00:09:14.701 --> 00:09:19.525
As a test administrator, change a user's
hire date and disable another user.

141
00:09:19.525 --> 00:09:20.800
Export the window.

142
00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:24.151
Then confirm the property
names against the real records,

143
00:09:24.151 --> 00:09:27.362
and correct any that differ
in your copy of the register.

144
00:09:27.362 --> 00:09:31.822
Write the tenant's real service
principal IDs into the copy and scan:

145
00:09:31.822 --> 00:09:35.326
both of your changes should
appear as second writers.

146
00:09:35.326 --> 00:09:36.890
Then back it all out.

147
00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:41.665
The dangers.

148
00:09:41.665 --> 00:09:46.131
Two writers of account state let a
late active value undo a disable;

149
00:09:46.131 --> 00:09:51.165
one writer per row, agreement across
rows, and the backstop warning guard it.

150
00:09:51.165 --> 00:09:56.196
A property name that doesn't match is
the quiet one: the scan reports nothing

151
00:09:56.196 --> 00:10:01.065
for that value, and silence looks clean,
so Lab 2 confirms every name first.

152
00:10:01.065 --> 00:10:04.890
Logs expire after thirty
days, so export on a schedule.

153
00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:08.227
A D writes for synced users
aren't in the Entra log.

154
00:10:08.227 --> 00:10:11.302
Deletion by the provisioning
app is caught twice.

155
00:10:11.302 --> 00:10:15.500
And the findings name people, so
keep them under access control.

156
00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:20.600
Here's what done looks like.

157
00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:24.988
The register file matches the signed
register, and the check reports nothing.

158
00:10:24.988 --> 00:10:28.972
Every writer group lists the
tenant's real service principal IDs,

159
00:10:28.972 --> 00:10:32.662
and every property name was
confirmed against a real record.

160
00:10:32.662 --> 00:10:36.051
The reader app holds exactly
the two read permissions.

161
00:10:36.051 --> 00:10:40.600
The scan runs on a schedule inside the
thirty days, into the evidence store.

162
00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:44.813
Every finding has an owner and
closes by removing the write path.

163
00:10:44.813 --> 00:10:48.826
And A D change auditing covers
every population still synced.

164
00:10:48.826 --> 00:10:53.487
Next, Chapter 2: contractor
sources and enforced end dates.

165
00:10:53.487 --> 00:10:54.650
Thanks for watching.
